=======================================================================
4. WHAT I BELIEVE THE REVIEWER GOT WRONG (not applied)
=======================================================================
a) ne-a #1's diagnosis of the 004 link. The reviewer says "the visible article number is still 10"
   and asks for "धारा 3" alone. But the English source labels the same URL "Article 10 of the
   Value-Added and Non-Value-Added Business Tax Act", so silently replacing 10 with 3 would both
   contradict the source and drop a digit the checker counts. Applied the campaign's existing
   cross-locale compromise instead, which states both numbers.

b) ne-a #2. The reviewer calls the missing degree a defect ("This is advice about the attorney's
   work, not a firm win-rate claim"). RULEBOOK R3 disagrees: that exact sentence is one of the three
   named advertising-rule sentences in 003 and its softening is deliberate. Not restored.

c) ne-a #3. The Chinese gloss on Korea's Medical Service Act is not a Taiwanese-term gloss misapplied
   — 醫療法 is the statute's own hanja and comes straight from the Chinese source. See §3.

d) ne-b #4's extension to columns-ne/001. 001 has no occurrence of the statute name; what it has is
   "विदेशी राष्ट्रियता भएका जिम्मेवार व्यक्ति (負責人)", where राष्ट्रियता is the ordinary Nepali noun
   "nationality" and is the corpus's own term (007 uses it four times, unflagged). Left as is.

e) ne-a #29's wider list. "…भेटमा बोलाउन थाले" and "…कोरियामा … भत्ता दिनुपर्छ" did need a danda and
   got one. "…10 वर्षभन्दा बढीदेखि कार्यरत थिए" is followed by "र … विक्रेता थिए।" — it is the first
   half of a coordinated pair, and the Korean source line likewise has no stop. A danda there would
   break the coordination.

f) ne-b #12's suggested rewrite drops one of the two digits ("अगाडिका 2 सवारी"), which would fail the
   checker's number parity. Kept both digits while making the same grammatical repair.


